event: section
data: {"type":"section","phase":"writer","sectionIndex":1,"sectionTitle":"Understanding Garmin Fish Finder Mounting Patterns: AMPS, Ball Sizes, and Compatibility","sectionContent":"## Understanding Garmin Fish Finder Mounting Patterns: AMPS, Ball Sizes, and Compatibility\n\nYou've picked out your Garmin ECHOMAP or Striker series unit, and now you're staring at the back of it wondering how this thing actually attaches to anything. Here's the good news: most Garmin fish finders use what's called an AMPS hole pattern on their mounting brackets. AMPS (short for Amps Mounting Pattern Standard) is a universal four-hole rectangular pattern that has become the industry standard for electronics mounting. This means your Garmin ECHOMAP AMPS plate isn't locking you into a single brand's ecosystem. You have options.\n\nThe next thing to understand is ball size. A fish finder AMPS mounting plate will typically feature either a 25mm (1-inch, also called \"B size\") or 38mm (1.5-inch, \"C size\") ball. Smaller, lighter units like the Garmin Striker 4 do well on a 25mm system, while larger ECHOMAP screens with more weight benefit from the extra stability of a 38mm ball and socket.\n\nThis is where Garmin ball mount compatibility really matters. iBOLT mounts use these same industry-standard ball sizes, so they're cross-compatible with RAM setups and other 25mm or 38mm systems you may already have on your boat. The [iBOLT 25mm Fish Finder Mounting Plate](https://iboltmounts.com/products/ibolt-25mm-1-inch-ball-composite-universal-marine-fish-finder-mounting-plate) and [iBOLT 38mm Fish Finder Mounting Plate](https://iboltmounts.com/products/ibolt-38mm-1-5-inch-ball-composite-universal-marine-fish-finder-mounting-plate) both connect directly to standard ball arms, letting you build or expand your setup without replacing everything you already own."}

event: status
data: {"type":"status","phase":"writer","message":"Writing section 3/7: \"Best Garmin Striker 4 and Striker Vivid Mounts: Rail and Handlebar Options\""}

event: section
data: {"type":"section","phase":"writer","sectionIndex":2,"sectionTitle":"Best Garmin Striker 4 and Striker Vivid Mounts: Rail and Handlebar Options","sectionContent":"## Best Garmin Striker 4 and Striker Vivid Mounts: Rail and Handlebar Options\n\nYou just rigged up your kayak, paddled out to your favorite spot, and now you're trying to balance a Garmin Striker 4 on your lap while managing your rod. Not exactly the relaxing morning on the water you had in mind. If you fish from a kayak, Jon boat, or any small watercraft where drilling holes isn't practical (or welcome), a rail or handlebar clamp mount is the way to go.\n\niBOLT builds Garmin Striker 4 fish finder mounts specifically for these situations, and there are three solid options depending on how much adjustability and stability you need.\n\nThe [iBOLT Garmin Striker 4 / Fish Finder Handlebar, Rail Mount](https://iboltmounts.com/products/ibolt-garmin-striker-4-fish-finder-handlebar-rail-mount) is the simplest setup. A single arm clamp that locks onto rails or tracks and holds your Striker 4 or Striker Vivid securely in place. It works well for calm water conditions where you don't need a lot of reach or repositioning.\n\nFor rougher water or setups where you need more extension, the [iBOLT Garmin Striker 4 / Fish Finder Dual Arm Handlebar, Rail Mount](https://iboltmounts.com/products/ibolt-garmin-striker-4-fish-finder-dual-arm-handlebar-rail-mount) adds a second arm segment. That extra joint gives you more reach and better vibration absorption, keeping your display steady when waves pick up.\n\nThen there's the [iBOLT Garmin Striker 4 / Fish Finder IncrediBOLT 360 Clamp / Handlebar / Rail Mount](https://iboltmounts.com/products/ibolt-garmin-striker-4-fish-finder-incredibolt-360-clamp-handlebar-rail-mount), which is the best Garmin fish finder mount option for anglers who constantly reposition their screen. The IncrediBOLT 360 base allows full rotational adjustment, so you can swing your Striker Vivid mount toward the cockpit while paddling, then rotate it to face your casting side once you're on the fish. No loosening knobs, no fiddling with angles mid-drift.\n\nAll three mounts use an AMPS pattern connection to your Garmin Striker 4, and every clamp fits standard rail and handlebar diameters found on kayak tracks, pontoon rails, and Jon boat framing. No drilling, no permanent modifications. You clamp on, tighten down, and fish.\n\nCheck out the full [Garmin Striker 4 clamp mount lineup](https://iboltmounts.com/products/ibolt-garmin-striker-4-fish-finder-incredibolt-360-clamp-handlebar-rail-mount) to see which setup matches the way you fish."}

